  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 944 AM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: For the weekend, cyclonic northwest flow will prevail \nunder an upper level low. However, for today, the region will be in \nan area of subsidence between shortwaves so it will be mostly dry, \nthough a couple upslope flurries will be possible in the mountains. \nA potent embedded shortwave will pass south tomorrow and tomorrow \nnight, bringing widespread showers. While they will be most \nconcentrated in the typical northwest flow upslope areas, very \nmarginal instability will develop in the lower 10,000 ft in the \natmosphere due to cold air aloft and solar heating. This will cause \nthe precipitation to trend more convective, especially Saturday \nafternoon. Therefore, snow showers will be able to survive crossing \nthe terrain and should pass through the downslope areas like the \nConnecticut River Valley, though likely in a lighter form. While \ntemperatures will rise above freezing below 2,000 ft, and likely \nreach 40 in the lowest points, temperatures will drop significantly \nin any of the showers. Therefore, even though consistent snow levels \nwill likely only be down to around 1,000-1,500 ft, a period of \ngraupel or even brief wet snow will be possible all the way down \ninto the valleys. However, any continuous accumulations should \ngenerally be limited to above 2,000 ft. The showers will gradually \nbecome less and less widespread heading into Sunday, and they should \nbe done by Sunday evening. Accumulations by the end should be in the \n1-4 inch range above 2,500 ft, with mostly brief coatings that melt \nbelow that. The lowest accumulating snow will fall Saturday night. \nMoisture off Lake Champlain will enhance the showers in the parts of \nthe Champlain Valley and Greens, but the more convective nature may \nprevent the formation of distinct bands.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Deterministic and ensemble guidance are in strong \nagreement that an 2-3 standard deviation ridge of high pressure will \nbe centered to the south and west of the North Country next week. \nTemperatures will start seasonably mild with highs in the 40s on \nFriday with temperatures warming a few degrees each day. Highs by \nFriday will warm into the mid 50s which will be 3-7 degrees above \nnormal. Some of the deterministic guidance is trying to flatten the \nridge on Friday with a strong shortwave but the likelihood of this \nscenario seems pretty low considering just how strong and amplified \nthe upper level ridge is forecasted to be. Should the ridge break \ndown, then it's possible we see some rain chances Friday into next \nweekend but again that's a very low confidence scenario as dry \nweather seems the most likely case at this time.",
